278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 191 0 R
/E (Karaoke is widely understood as more than entertainment; it is also a social and cultural practice. Research in game and play studies describes karaoke as a form of social performance play that is easily adapted into different communities and settings, highlighting its role in participation, shared experience, and performative interaction \(Bryan & Tanenbaum, 2019\). At the same time, karaoke is emotionally complex. A clinical study on karaoke therapy found evidence of improved social interaction in some participants, while also indicating that karaoke could be linked to anxiety in certain situations, which is especially relevant in public or semi-public contexts where users may feel judged or exposed \(Leung et al., 1998\). This tention between enjoyment and vulnerability makes karaoke a valuable design context for exploring how interfaces and environments can better support comfort, inclusion, and engagement \(Bryan & Tanenbaum, 2019; Leung et al., 1998\). )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
